<article class="env-lemma" id="0FBE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FBE">Lemma <span data-tag="0FBE">42.52.5</span>.</a> Let $(S, \delta )$ be as in Situation <a href="/tag/02QL" data-tag="02QL">42.7.1</a>. Let $X$ be locally of finite type over $S$. Let $Z \to X$ be a closed immersion. Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  E_1 \to E_2 \to E_3 \to E_1[1]  \]
</div>
  <p> be a distinguished triangle of perfect objects in $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
the restrictions $E_1|_{X \setminus Z}$ and $E_3|_{X \setminus Z}$ are zero, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
at least one of the following is true: (a) $X$ is quasi-compact, (b) $X$ has quasi-compact irreducible components, (c) $E_3 \to E_1[1]$ can be represented by a map of locally bounded complexes of finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules, or (d) there exists an envelope $f : Y \to X$ such that $Lf^*E_3 \to Lf^*E_1[1]$ can be represented by a map of locally bounded complexes of finite locally free $\mathcal{O}_ Y$-modules. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  P_ p(Z \to X, E_2) = P_ p(Z \to X, E_1) + P_ p(Z \to X, E_3)  \]
</div>
  <p> for all $p \in \mathbf{Z}$ and consequently $ch(Z \to X, E_2) = ch(Z \to X, E_1) + ch(Z \to X, E_3)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The proof is exactly the same as the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FBD" data-tag="0FBD">42.52.4</a> except it uses Lemma <a href="/tag/0FAZ" data-tag="0FAZ">42.49.8</a> at the very end. For $p > 0$ we can deduce this lemma from Lemma <a href="/tag/0FBD" data-tag="0FBD">42.52.4</a> with $p_1 = p_3 = 1$ and the relationship between $P_ p(Z \to X, E)$ and $c_ p(Z \to X, E)$ given in Lemma <a href="/tag/0FBA" data-tag="0FBA">42.52.1</a>. The case $p = 0$ can be shown directly (it is only interesting if $X$ has a connected component entirely contained in $Z$). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>